[[!comment format=mdwn
 username="joey"
 subject="""comment 4"""
 date="2026-11-10T13:02:49Z"
 content="""
This is a harder problem than I thought.

---

There is currently no way to promptly and fully stop all helper processes
belonging to an AnnexState (or an AnnexRead). Two examples of processes
that are not stopped by stopCoProcesses are external special remotes and
external backends.

Similarly, there is no way to close every file handle that might be held
open by an AnnexState. Eg, a Remote might open a Handle and keep it open.

This could be fixed, but it would be a lot of work and add rarely used code
paths in multiple Remotes, etc. Which, if they're buggy, shows up as
p2phttp using more resources than expected for a period of time.

Letting the AnnexState get garbage collected will stop all helper processes
and close all file handles, but only once the GC runs. If new AnnexStates
are being spawned faster than they're being forgotten, that could result
in more helper processes and file handles being allocated than expected.
So relying on GC doesn't seem feasible, unless the GC is forced to run
at a particular point (System.Mem.performBlockingMajorGC). Forcing GC
runs seems dodgy and might hurt performance?

---

When dupState is used to create a pool of AnnexStates that all cooperate
on one repository, stopCoProcesses on any of them stops the helper 
processes of all of them. There is no way to only stop 1 of each type of
helper process. So it's not possible to cool down only one of the
AnnexStates that has not been recently used, while leaving the rest warmed
up. This is particularly a problem for the cat-file process, which is
shared amoung all AnnexStates in the pool, and so can't possibly be stopped
without cooling down the whole pool. (It might be solvable for the other
helper processes.)

If dupState is not used and AnnexStates are fully independent,
then stopCoProcesses can be used to cool one down, but then each time one
is warmed up it has to restart everything.

---

Here are two approaches that avoid the problems of stopCoProcesses:

1. Bounded pool of PerRepoServerStates. When starting a new one,
   cool down all the AnnexStates in the pool for the LRU one.
   (Which stopCoProcesses will do just fine.)
2. No dupState, so all AnnexStates are fully independent.
   Before using an AnnexState, if there are too many warmed up,
   find the LRU one and cool it down.

With #1, there would need to be another option, like
--max-concurrent-repos. The maximum number of running helper
processes would be on the order of that times --jobs.
(Though it can be much less, eg when all traffic is going to a few
repositories and other repositories are not being used.)

With #2, the total number of running helper processes
would be on the order of --jobs.

The benefit of #1 is that often the same few repos will get most
of the traffic. In that case, helper processes get started once
and used for a long time even when the server is fully loaded.

#1 has an edge case with very bad behavior: A fully loaded server
is serving several requests for frequently used repos and 1 request
for a little-used repo comes in. That request needs to block
until all currently active requests for one of the active repos
are complete. And for fairness, while that request is blocked,
no new requests can be started that would keep an active repo active.
And, once that request is running, new requests for the now inactive
repo also have to block.

With #2, a fully loaded server will frequently have the 
LRU AnnexState getting cooled down, and then soon after warmed up
and needing to restart everything. That will increase CPU load when the
server is fully loaded. But it won't cause any long-term stalls,
requests will always be handled in the order received.

So #2 seems better.
"""]]
